**CyberShield NIDS**
Experimental Network Intrusion Detection System — Dual-Guard ML Architecture

**Overview**
CyberShield is an experimental, research-oriented Network Intrusion Detection System (NIDS) that investigates how confidence threshold tuning affects detection reliability in a dual-model machine learning pipeline trained on imbalanced network traffic.
Rather than presenting a production-ready security system, this project focuses on:

-Systematic threshold sensitivity analysis using cross-validation
-Honest evaluation of supervised vs unsupervised anomaly detection
-Understanding when and why default thresholds fail on imbalanced data
-Interpretable dual-guard inference architecture

**Research Question**

How does confidence threshold tuning influence false-positive rates and detection stability in a dual-model intrusion detection system trained on imbalanced network traffic?


**Architecture — Dual-Guard Detection**

**Guard 1 — Random Forest**

-Trained on labeled flows, knows what past attacks look like
-Returns attack probability for every packet
-If probability ≥ θ → ATTACK, stop, flag immediately
-Precision 98% — when it says attack it is almost always right
-Weakness: misses attacks that look like normal traffic statistically

**Guard 2 — Isolation Forest**

-Trained on normal traffic only, never seen an attack label
-Learned what normal looks like, not what attacks look like
-Returns an anomaly score — more negative = more unusual
-Does not make a binary decision, score only
-Only evaluated on packets Guard 1 passed as normal

**Score interpretation**

-Below p10 → HIGH suspicion (bottom 10% most anomalous of normal pool)
-Between p10 and p25 → MEDIUM suspicion
-Above p25 → LOW, both guards agree, likely clean

**The interesting case**

Guard 1 says NORMAL + Guard 2 score is strongly negative = zero-day candidate
Guard 1 does not recognise it as a known pattern
Guard 2 says it looks nothing like normal traffic
Neither alone catches it, the combination flags it

**Dataset**
UNSW-NB15 — synthesised network traffic dataset from the Australian Centre for Cyber Security.
-Training set has 82,332 flows. 44.9% are normal traffic, 55.1% are attacks — so the training data is slightly attack-heavy, not the class imbalance direction most people assume when they hear "imbalanced dataset."
-Test set has 175,341 flows — more than double the training set. 31.9% normal, 68.1% attacks. The test set is more attack-heavy than training, which means the model is evaluated on a harder distribution than it was trained on.

45 raw features, 15 selected for modelling
Attack categories: Fuzzers, Exploits, DoS, Reconnaissance, Backdoor, Shellcode, Generic, Analysis
Binary label: 0 = Normal, 1 = Attack


No SMOTE — dataset imbalance is moderate (~55/45). class_weight='balanced' handles it without synthetic data that could distort the decision boundary
LabelEncoder fit on train only — prevents category code mismatch between train and test
IF trained on normal-only samples — learns normal traffic boundary, not the full distribution
Contamination = auto — avoids the hard limit issue; decision_function() scores used at inference, not predict()


Threshold Sensitivity Analysis
The core research contribution. Confidence threshold directly controls the precision/recall tradeoff in binary classification.
Method

Split training data into 5 stratified folds
For each fold: train RF, collect validation probabilities, sweep θ from 0.10 to 0.85
Average F1 across folds per threshold → select best θ
Apply CV-selected threshold to held-out test set once

Test set F1 at default θ=0.50: 92.94%
Test set F1 at CV-optimal θ=0.45: 93.32% (+0.38%)

Threshold was selected on validation folds only. Test set used once for final reporting.

**Finding**
Default thresholds are suboptimal for imbalanced intrusion detection. A well-tuned threshold on a moderately complex model beats a default threshold on a more complex model — and the tuning cost is near zero compared to architectural changes.

**Results**

Guard 1 — Random Forest (Default θ=0.50)
MetricValueAccuracy90.88%Precision98.28%Recall88.15%F1-Score92.94%ROC-AUC0.9856PR-AUC0.9925Missed Attacks (FN)14,145 (11.9%)False Alarms (FP)1,840 (3.3%)
Threshold Optimisation (CV-Validated)
ValueDefault θ=0.50 F192.94%CV-Optimal θ0.45Optimised F193.32%Improvement+0.38%CV Mean F1 at θ=0.4594.59% ± 0.15% (5-fold)
Methodology: Threshold selected using 5-fold StratifiedKFold on training data only. The optimal threshold was applied to the held-out test set once. The test set was never used during threshold selection — the improvement claim is not inflated by test-set optimisation.
**Key Finding**
-Default threshold (θ=0.50) is suboptimal for this task. The CV-selected threshold (θ=0.45) improves F1 by +0.38% by better balancing the precision-recall tradeoff on imbalanced network traffic. The improvement is modest but reproducible and methodologically sound — consistent with literature on threshold calibration in imbalanced classification.
-The CV sweep also reveals that F1 is relatively stable between θ=0.40 and θ=0.55 (all within 0.002 of each other), which means the system is not highly sensitive to small threshold changes in this range. Below θ=0.30 recall increases sharply but precision drops significantly.

**Dual-Guard System**
**Guard 2** contribution at p10 cutoff: Recovered 1,039 attacks (7.3% of Guard 1 misses) at the cost of 5,791 new false alarms. Recovery ratio: 0.18 attacks per false alarm.
This confirms that IF as a hard binary second-stage filter is not beneficial on this dataset — the feature space overlap between normal and attack traffic is too high. Guard 2 is therefore retained as an anomaly score signal only, not a decision layer.

**Five-tab interactive interface:**
-Real-Time Detection: Adjust packet sliders or pick an attack template. Guard 1 gives ATTACK/NORMAL + confidence.Guard 2 gives anomaly score + suspicion level (LOW/MEDIUM/HIGH). Combined interpretation flags zero-day candidates.
-Threshold Tuning: Adjust θ live. See CV-optimal value and how the current setting compares. Changes apply immediately to all inference.
-Batch Analysis: Upload a CSV of network flows. Real model runs inference on every row. Returns RF prediction, probability, IF score, and suspicion level per row. Downloads full results CSV.
-Error Analysis: Loads real metrics from the saved model package. Shows confusion matrix, threshold sensitivity curve (CV-validated), ROC curve, and IF score distribution. No hardcoded numbers.
-System Information: Architecture diagram, guard descriptions, research finding summary, honest limitations.

Limitations
These are intentional constraints, not oversights:

-No live packet capture — demo interface only, not a production NIDS
-Single dataset — thresholds optimised for UNSW-NB15 may not generalise to other networks
-Binary classification only — attack categories are inferred from rules, not from a multiclass model
-IF as score signal only — Isolation Forest was evaluated as a binary second-stage filter and found to introduce an unfavourable false-alarm-to-recovery ratio on this dataset (feature space overlap too high). It is retained as an anomaly score indicator
-Simulated traffic only — attack templates use hand-crafted parameter values, not real captured traffic




Tech Stack
Pandas, NumPy,Scikit-learn (RandomForestClassifier, IsolationForest),Scikit-learn metrics, cross-validation,Matplotlib, Seaborn, Gradio,Joblib
